The search for ""getSelection" table "parentTable" office.js Word.run" returned the following summary:
To get the parent table of the current user selection in Word using Office.js (`Word.run`), you can retrieve the selection's range and access its **`parentTableCell`** (or **`parentTable`** depending on where the cursor/selection lies)[1][2].

Because the selection might not be inside a table, it is best practice to use the **`*OrNullObject`** versions to prevent your script from throwing an `ItemNotFound` error[1].

### Example Code

```javascript
await Word.run(async (context) => {
    // 1. Get the current selection range
    const selection = context.document.getSelection();

    // 2. Attempt to get the parent table cell or parent table safely
    // Note: parentTableCellOrNullObject is available starting from WordApi 1.3
    const parentCell = selection.parentTableCellOrNullObject;
    parentCell.load("isNullObject");

    await context.sync();

    if (!parentCell.isNullObject) {
        // The selection is inside a table cell. 
        // Now you can grab the parent table from that cell:
        const parentTable = parentCell.parentTable;

        // Load properties you need from the table (e.g., alignment, style)
        parentTable.load("styleBuiltIn, rowIndex, cellIndex");

        await context.sync();

        console.log("Found parent table!");
    } else {
        console.log("The current selection is not inside a table.");
    }
});
```

### Alternative: Checking `parentTable` directly from a Range
Depending on your exact requirement (e.g., if the selection spans across parts of a table or boundary lines), a `Range` object also supports navigating context properties[2]. However, checking `parentTableCellOrNullObject` first is the most reliable approach for cell-level cursor placements or text selections inside tables.

