In the Word JavaScript API (Office.js), the **`Word.Alignment`** enum specifies the horizontal alignment options for paragraphs[1]. 

The available enum values (along with their string representations) are[1]:

| Enum Constant | String Value | Description[1] |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `Word.Alignment.centered` | `"Centered"` | Alignment to the center. |
| `Word.Alignment.justified` | `"Justified"` | Fully justified alignment. |
| `Word.Alignment.left` | `"Left"` | Alignment to the left. |
| `Word.Alignment.right` | `"Right"` | Alignment to the right. |
| `Word.Alignment.mixed` | `"Mixed"` | Mixed horizontal alignment. |
| `Word.Alignment.unknown` | `"Unknown"` | Unknown alignment (typically read-only/fallback). |

### Example Usage:
```javascript
await Word.run(async (context) => {
    const paragraph = context.document.body.paragraphs.getFirst();

    // Using the enum
    paragraph.alignment = Word.Alignment.centered;

    // Alternatively, using the string literal value
    // paragraph.alignment = "Centered";

    await context.sync();
});
```

