In the Word JavaScript API (Office.js), when you want to apply the standard **"Table Grid"** style to a table dynamically—ensuring that it remains locale-independent—you should use the **`styleBuiltIn`** property mapped to the `Word.BuiltInStyleName.tableGrid` enum (or string literal `"TableGrid"`) instead of relying on localized style name strings[1][2].

### Code Example

```javascript
await Word.run(async (context) => {
    // 1. Insert sample table data
    const data = [
        ["Header 1", "Header 2", "Header 3"],
        ["Data A", "Data B", "Data C"]
    ];
    const table: Word.Table = context.document.body.insertTable(2, 3, "Start", data);

    // 2. Apply the "Table Grid" built-in style
    table.styleBuiltIn = Word.BuiltInStyleName.tableGrid; 
    // Alternatively, you can use the literal string:
    // table.styleBuiltIn = "TableGrid";

    await context.sync();
});
```

### Key Considerations:
* **`styleBuiltIn` vs `style`**: Use `styleBuiltIn` with `"TableGrid"` (or `Word.BuiltInStyleName.tableGrid`) so your Office Add-in works properly across different language versions of Microsoft Word[1]. Use the standard `style` property only if you are referencing custom or user-localized style names[1].
* **API Requirements**: Requires **WordApi 1.3** or higher[2].

