In the context of the **Office JavaScript API (Office.js)** for Microsoft Word, working with tables, ranges, and text involves a few distinct objects and patterns[1][2][3]. 

Depending on what you are trying to achieve, here is how `table.getRange()`, text manipulation, and range interactions work in Word.js:

---

### 1. Getting a Range from a Table (`table.getRange()`)
The `Word.Table` class provides a `getRange()` method that returns a `Word.Range` object representing the table or parts of it[1].

```typescript
await Word.run(async (context) => {
    const table = context.document.body.tables.getFirst();

    // Get the range containing the whole table
    const tableRange = table.getRange(Word.RangeLocation.whole); // or pass "Whole"

    tableRange.load("text");
    await context.sync();

    console.log("Table text content:", tableRange.text);
});
```

* **Parameters for `getRange()`**: 
  * `Word.RangeLocation.whole` (or `"Whole"`) – Default. Gets the range enclosing the entire table[1].
  * `Word.RangeLocation.start` (or `"Start"`) – Gets a zero-length range at the start of the table[1].
  * `Word.RangeLocation.end` (or `"End"`) – Gets a zero-length range at the end of the table[1].
  * `Word.RangeLocation.after` (or `"After"`) – Gets a range immediately following the table[1].

---

