In Office.js for Word, working with table columns, iterating through their `items`, checking or getting their `width`, and setting column widths involves specific objects and methods like `tableColumns`, `load()`, and `setWidth()`[1][2][3].

Below is an overview and practical example of how to use these properties and methods.

### 1. Accessing and Reading Column Widths
To read column widths using `tableColumns` and `items`, you must load the `width` property using `.load()` and call `context.sync()` before accessing them[1]:

```javascript
await Word.run(async (context) => {
    // Get the first table in the document
    const table = context.document.body.tables.getFirst();

    // Get the columns collection from the table range
    const tableColumns = table.getRange().tableColumns;

    // Load properties including width for each item in the collection
    tableColumns.load("items/columnIndex, items/width");
    await context.sync();

    // Iterate through items
    tableColumns.items.forEach((column) => {
        console.log(`Column ${column.columnIndex} width is ${column.width} points.`);
    });
});
```

### 2. Setting Column Widths (`setWidth`)
The `Word.TableColumnCollection` object provides a `setWidth(columnWidth, rulerStyle)` method to adjust widths across columns[3]:

* **`columnWidth`**: The desired width in points (`number`).
* **`rulerStyle`**: Determines how scaling behaves (e.g., `Word.RulerStyle.points` or other applicable enumerations).

Example of setting widths on a column collection[3]:
```javascript
await Word.run(async (context) => {
    const table = context.document.body.tables.getFirst();
    const tableColumns = table.getRange().tableColumns;

    // Load collection items if necessary, or directly apply setWidth
    tableColumns.setWidth(72, Word.RulerStyle.points); // Sets columns to 1 inch (72 points)

    await context.sync();
    console.log("Table column widths updated.");
});
```

*(Note: Individual `Word.TableColumn` elements also support preferred sizing via properties like `preferredWidth` and `preferredWidthType` depending on your precise layout requirements[1].)*

